---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/url";

const local = `# Replay a log you saved from a failed job — no CI, no token, no LLM:
ci-doctor analyze ~/Downloads/failing-job.log

# Or hit a live run from your checkout. With no CI_PROJECT_ID / GITHUB_REPOSITORY
# set, the repo is read from your git origin (and it says so in a warning):
ci-doctor analyze 123456`;

const docker = `# Build once where there is internet, push to your internal registry:
docker build -t ci-doctor:latest .
docker tag  ci-doctor:latest registry.internal.example.com/ci-doctor:latest
docker push registry.internal.example.com/ci-doctor:latest

# At job runtime the image reaches only your GitLab/LLM endpoints and the
# matcher registries you declare.`;

const offline = `# Where there IS internet — build a wheel bundle:
pip wheel . -w ./wheels

# Copy ./wheels inside the air-gapped network, then:
pip install --no-index --find-links ./wheels ci-doctorr`;

const localRegistry = `# Where there IS internet:
git clone https://github.com/ci-doctor/matchers.git

# Inside, point official at the copy (the folder holding index.yaml):
ci-doctor matchers registry add official=./matchers
ci-doctor matchers add official/python
ci-doctor config --validate`;
---

<Base
    title="Local & air-gapped"
    description="Run ci-doctor from your laptop, or ship it into a network with no egress."
>
    <h1>Local &amp; air-gapped</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Nothing about ci-doctor requires CI, and nothing about it requires the
        public internet: keep the matcher registry local and it reaches only the
        hosts you run yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>On your laptop</h2>
    <p>
        The fastest way to test a <code>.ci-doctor.yml</code> or reproduce an attribution
        you disagree with:
    </p>
    <Code code={local} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
        Passing a path that exists means no run is fetched — see
        <a href={url("/start/first-run/")}>First run</a>. The selected matchers are
        still read from their registries.
    </p>

    <h2>Ship it as an image</h2>
    <p>Build a self-contained image and push it to an internal registry:</p>
    <Code code={docker} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />

    <h2 id="offline">…or as an offline wheel bundle</h2>
    <p>Where Docker isn't an option:</p>
    <Code code={offline} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />

    <h2>A local matcher registry</h2>
    <p>
        Matchers are read from their registries on every run, with no cache. Clone
        the registry where there is internet, carry it in, and point the alias at
        the copy; a relative path resolves against the config file that names it:
    </p>
    <Code code={localRegistry} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
        To update, pull or copy a newer revision. Nothing else is fetched.
    </p>

    <h2>What it still needs to reach</h2>
    <ul class="tight">
        <li>
            Your GitLab or GitHub API host — unless you replay a saved log, in
            which case nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
            Your LLM endpoint, if you configured one. Skip it and the
            deterministic report is the output.
        </li>
        <li>
            Each matcher registry you declare — none, if it is a local path.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Nothing else. No telemetry, no update checks, no model downloads. The
        <code>official</code> registry's address,
        <code>https://matchers.cidoc.dev/</code>, is the one shipped default host,
        and you replace it as above. The one thing to avoid in a strict air
        gap is the <code>litellm</code> backend: it may pull <code
            >tiktoken</code
        >, which fetches its vocabulary at runtime.
        <a href={url("/start/requirements/")}>Requirements</a> has the full
        network story.
    </p>
</Base>
